# PyCharm
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/

### Gradle, for the JetBrains plugin under editors/jetbrains/ ###
#
# `build/` and `lib/` in the Python section above already cover Gradle's output directory
# and would swallow any directory named lib/, which is why the plugin keeps its sources
# under src/main/java and has no lib/ of its own. These three are what Gradle adds that
# nothing above catches.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored, and this is the sharp one:
# editors/jetbrains/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar. It is conventionally committed --
# a wrapper without its jar cannot bootstrap, so `./gradlew` fails on a clean checkout and
# the CI job cannot run -- and it is conventionally gitignored, because a wildcard on *.jar
# catches it. There is no *.jar rule in this file today. If one is ever added, it needs a
# negation for that path, for the same reason packaging/rpm/ash.spec needed one: `git add`
# skips ignored files SILENTLY, so the wrapper would be written, verified against, and lost
# with no error anywhere.
editors/jetbrains/.gradle/
editors/jetbrains/local.properties
editors/jetbrains/.kotlin/
# The IntelliJ Platform Gradle plugin's own cache of JetBrains product-release listings.
# It writes .json and .json.lock files here on every resolve, and they are machine-local and
# time-dependent, so committing them would make the tree dirty after any build.
editors/jetbrains/.intellijPlatform/

